---
// WR-62: phone-number guide. Draft: docs/marketing/seo/content/who-is-selling-my-phone-number.md (seo-growth).
// Sources table stripped into the comments below. Clearance: docs/research/timeline-claims-2026-11.md part 1, WR-62
// (Priya Shah, 2026-11-08). Draft changes: S2 "sites usually remove the whole profile" was NOT cleared, replaced with the
// research doc's per-listing wording; S1 "often brings up" -> "can bring up" (no primary source); S4 uses the FTC wording
// from the research doc; "in a few months" -> "now and then" (the interval is advice, not a rule). Copy rules: POSITIONING.md.
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHead from "@/components/PageHead.astro";
import Alert from "@/components/ui/Alert.astro";
import Card from "@/components/ui/Card.astro";
import Icon from "@/components/Icon.astro";
import CtaDownload from "@/components/CtaDownload.astro";
import { pageProps, pages, url, type Crumb } from "@/lib/site";
const p = pageProps("phone-number");
const crumbs: Crumb[] = [["Home", ""], [pages.guides.label, pages.guides.out], [pages["phone-number"].label, p.out]];
---
<Base {...p} crumbs={crumbs}>
<PageHead crumbs={crumbs} title="Get your phone number off people-search sites"
  lead="Typing a phone number into a people-search site can bring up a name and address. Here are the free steps to ask each site to take that listing down.">
  <picture slot="art">
    {/* source: brand/illustrations/phone-number-*.svg (graphic-designer), alt from brand/illustrations/README.md */}
    <source srcset={url("assets/art/phone-number-dark.svg")} media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <img src={url("assets/art/phone-number-light.svg")} width="320" height="250" class="h-auto w-full"
      alt="A phone with its number hidden and a green check." />
  </picture>
</PageHead>
{/* source: research doc WR-62 S1 ("often" -> "can", no primary source for frequency); what the sites sell = faq.yaml who-buys (ODNI report) */}
<div class="wrap-narrow pb-2xl">
  <h2 id="why">Why is my number there?</h2>
  {/* source: research doc WR-62 S1: no primary source for where numbers come from, so the draft's hedge is kept and the list is softened to "can" */}
  <p class="mt-s">People-search sites build profiles from public records and other data, and link them by details like a phone number. Numbers can reach these sites from public records, marketing lists and other sources. We don't claim to know which source put your number on a given site.</p>

  <h2 class="mt-xl" id="steps">How do I get it taken down?</h2>
  {/* source: steps = our guides (brokers/registry.sample.yaml); per-listing = research doc WR-62 S2 suggested wording (Spokeo: each
     listing is a separate request; BeenVerified: one record online); relisting = research doc WR-61 S2 / WR-62 S3 CLEARED
     (Spokeo Compass, CPPA "How DROP works"); removal mailbox = PLAN_V1 D2; never pay/SSN = shared rule 6, POSITIONING rule 4 */}
  <ol class="mt-s list-decimal ps-m">
    <li>Search your own number and name, and write down each site that lists you.</li>
    <li>Use each site's own removal page. Our step-by-step guides cover <a href={url("guides/spokeo/")}>Spokeo</a>, <a href={url("guides/whitepages/")}>Whitepages</a>, <a href={url("guides/truepeoplesearch/")}>TruePeopleSearch</a> and <a href={url("guides/")}>more sites</a>.</li>
    <li>Remove the listing that shows your number. Most removal pages ask you to pick that listing and remove it. Each listing is a separate request, and a site can have more than one listing for you.</li>
    <li>Use an email address just for removals. You should never have to give a card number or a Social Security number.</li>
    <li>Check again now and then. Some sites can list people again under a new record.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 class="mt-xl" id="calls">Will this stop the calls?</h2>
  {/* source: "does not touch your carrier" = a limit, no source needed (research doc). S4 CLEARED: FTC "National Do Not Call Registry FAQs"
     https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/national-do-not-call-registry-faqs (accessed 2026-11-08): stops sales calls from companies that follow
     the law, not scammers; political, charity, survey and debt-collection calls still allowed; up to 31 days; report at donotcall.gov,
     money lost to a scam at reportfraud.ftc.gov */}
  <p class="mt-s">Not on its own. Removing listings doesn't change anything with your phone company or your phone.</p>
  <Alert variant="note" icon="phone" class="mt-s"><p>The FTC's Do Not Call registry stops sales calls from companies that follow the law. It won't stop scammers, and political, charity, survey and debt-collection calls are still allowed. It can take up to 31 days to take effect. Report unwanted calls at <a href="https://www.donotcall.gov/">donotcall.gov</a>. If you lost money to a scam, report it at <a href="https://reportfraud.ftc.gov/">reportfraud.ftc.gov</a>.</p></Alert>

  <Card as="section" class="reveal mt-xl p-l shadow-2" aria-labelledby="auto">
    <h2 id="auto" class="flex items-center gap-xs"><Icon name="shield" class="size-8 text-brand" />Rather not do it all by hand?</h2>
    {/* source: LICENSE (free, AGPL); PLAN_V1 D1/D4 (sends requests, keeps checking); phone only with permission = tiers.py/base.py;
       no account = ROADMAP principle 6; scope = POSITIONING.md rule 6 */}
    <p class="mt-s">Free My ID is a free, open-source Windows app that sends the removal requests for you and keeps checking. There's no account. It works on people-search and data-broker sites only, and it gives a site your phone number only if you switch that on.</p>
    <div class="mt-s flex flex-wrap items-center gap-s">
      <CtaDownload size="md" />
      <a href={url("how-it-works/")}>See how it works</a>
    </div>
  </Card>
</div>
</Base>
